into a coffee house type environment, and they play gentle music, and the people with dementia typically love music. They may respond to music in ways they don't to other communication, and the caregivers have a chance to be together. So there's tons of innovations that we can do, and that we need to be doing much more of. Now, you've been studying caregiving for decades. How did you first get interested in this field? This was actually, I can date it to 1981. So I had just finished my psychology internship at the University of Washington, Seattle, and I started a postdoctoral fellowship in geriatric clinical psychology.
进入咖啡馆式的环境，他们播放柔和的音乐，痴呆症患者通常非常喜欢音乐。他们对音乐的反应可能会超过对其他沟通方式的反应，而照顾者也有机会聚在一起。因此，我们可以做、并且需要做更多的创新有成千上万种。现在，你研究照顾工作已经几十年了。你最初是怎么对这个领域产生兴趣的？这其实可以追溯到 1981 年。当时我刚完成在西雅图华盛顿大学的心理学实习，并开始了一项老年临床心理学博士后研究。

31
00:21:47.000 --> 00:22:30.000
And I'll confess the reason that I started that postdoctoral fellowship was not out of a deep love and interest of geriatrics, but I went on the academic job market and applied for 22 jobs and didn't. get one. So I needed to make a fresh start. And so my first clinic assignment was in a unit called an outpatient clinic called the Geriatric and Family Services Clinic. And it was one of the first dementia clinics in the United States. This is at the University of Washington, Seattle. And my primary job was to go and do psychological testing. Psychologists play a key role in assessing memory, intelligence, visual spatial skills, et cetera. And that was my main job.
我坦白说，我开始那个博士后研究并不是出于对老年学的一往情深和热爱，而是因为我进入了学术就业市场，申请了 22 个工作却一个都没拿到。我需要重新开始。因此，我的第一个诊所分配是在一个名为老年与家庭服务诊所的门诊部。那是美国最早的痴呆症诊所之一。这是在西雅图华盛顿大学。我的主要工作是去进行心理测试。心理学家在评估记忆、智力、视觉空间技能等方面发挥着关键作用。那是我的主要工作，

32
00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:53.000
and they're still mainstays. But I found I was fascinated by the people who brought the person with dementia in, the caregivers. And at this time, 1981, there was no functioning Alzheimer's Association. There were no self-help groups. There were no books. But I found as a psychologist, I could teach people the ABCs, the antecedents of the behavior problems their relatives had,
而且它们仍然是支柱。但我发现我被那些把痴呆症患者带来的人——照顾者深深吸引了。在当时，即 1981 年，还没有运作良好的阿尔茨海默病协会。没有自助小组。没有书。但我发现作为一名心理学家，我可以教人们 ABC 法则，即他们的亲属所具有的行为问题的前因，

33
00:22:53.000 --> 00:23:36.000
understanding the behavior, how to respond differently to it, how to communicate. And I found I had some of the most. grateful clients I had ever worked with. These regular folks who had a spouse or a parent developed dementia and they were bewildered. And I found I could readily help them. So I made it my sort of mission in my career to go on with this. And I subsequently learned not only was it great working with these families to help them and the most gratifying work I've ever done. You see amazing examples of people providing care under very difficult circumstances, wonderful people. But I also really realized that it was a
理解行为，如何以不同的方式对此做出反应，如何进行沟通。我发现我拥有了一些我共事过的最心怀感激的客户。这些普通人，他们的配偶或父母患上了痴呆症，他们感到迷惑不解。我发现我能够轻而易举地帮助他们。所以我把这作为我职业生涯的一种使命继续下去。我随后了解到，不仅与这些家庭合作以帮助他们非常棒，这也是我做过的最令人欣慰的工作。你看到了在非常困难的环境下提供照顾的人们的惊人例子，都是些了不起的人。但我也真正意识到，这是一个

34
00:23:36.000 --> 00:24:22.000
great way to study psychological stress. So that's led us into studying stress process models and how those can predict well-being, how they might predict physical changes that people experience with caregiving, how we can use that to design interventions. So I've got to say it's been a great adventure. And then Beyond that, I began to realize the enormity of this issue of people with stroke, with cancer, with end-of-life issues, and they all face their unique problems. So it's a gratifying area, and I really believe every psychologist needs to know something about helping caregivers, because we're all going to see it in our practice. How does the United States compare to other countries regarding caregiving?
研究心理压力的绝佳途径。因此，这引导我们去研究压力过程模型以及它们如何能够预测福祉，它们如何能够预测人们在照顾过程中经历的身体变化，以及我们如何利用这一点来设计干预措施。所以我得说这是一次伟大的冒险。除此之外，我开始意识到中风患者、癌症患者和临终关怀问题这一巨大议题，他们都面临着各自独特的问题。因此，这是一个令人欣慰的领域，我真的相信每个心理学家都需要了解一些关于如何帮助照顾者的知识，因为我们都会在实践中遇到这种情况。美国在照顾方面与其他国家相比如何？

